r cubic meter this was real yeah  the school had to this is the poor  school were they going to be able to do  this or not so they designed this  project and wanted the kids to take two  approaches the first approach was isn't  google google earth wonderful all right  there's the school that's the satellite  image you can't see it down the corner  but there's a scale there  that says this far is 50 meters  that was the task  open is that but they anticipated in  their work solution here a whole series  of enabling prompts little bits of help  they might be able to give the kids  like um  this is an irregular shape here is a way  of dividing it into regular shapes  telling them what the scale factor was  instead of asking them to work it out i  think a lot of the art of good teaching  is the art of anticipation of what kids  are going to do so they anticipated this  stuff  the second way in which the kids had to  do it was to get a trundle wheel and go  outside  do some real-life measuring and again  there's a whole set of enabling fronts  here that the teachers had anticipated  where the students might need some more  help  and it turned out in the work solutions  that they had when you work out the cost  using the satellite image it's over 26  000 you could use them trundle wheels  about 24 000.  here are some of the extending prompts  they came up with  what if the school can only allocate 10  000  to the cost of repairs  how does that affect the depth  so you worked it out for 50 millimeters  you know what what could you afford to  put on for ten thousand dollars and is  it worth doing  a really nice second extending prompt is  essentially saying generalize  create a general model for working this  out  and then the third one all right you've  done it two ways  um  which method do you think is the best  method  and why  would you think that  so  that's just an example of how they  designed a quite a big a big task that  thought about ways in which they could  help all those diverse students get  through it and extend the ones who  finished early and they had a huge range  of achievement levels in this school it  was really worrying the teachers the  other thing that they why they joined  the project was that most of the kids  hated maths  they hated it they didn't want to do it  so they were trying to devise some  mathematical activities that were real  for the students as well  all right that's how i've tried to  answer those questions but let me just  come to a final word  differentiating the curriculum in the  way that i've just described is hard  this is not an easy thing for us to do  because it requires significant teacher  knowledge and skills  and time peace  so just think about the demands on you  you are qualified teachers what about  those teachers at bungalow  who didn't have the advantages that you  had of being taught on june apps  so it's hard but i think there are at  least two reasons why it's important  that we as a nation rise to this  challenge the first one goes back to the  equity considerations that i mentioned  at the start i find it completely  unacceptable that  diversity is associated with different  levels of mathematical achievement i  think that's a lot on our country the  second reason again as judy mentioned at  the start i didn't know what she was  going to say it was wonderful  but we  teacher educators and teachers are under  intense  pressure and scrutiny to justify our  professional status and our professional  practice  leon talked about the project that we're  doing with money that came from the  previous government it was the chief  scientist who instigated that because  he's been highly critical of the quality  of maths and science teaching and  learning in australian schools  you know you see politicians you see the  media commentators constantly  criticizing us  and what we do and yes  christopher pines inquiry into teacher  education the issues paper came out last  week  and somehow we have to respond to this  thing  so i think we can respond in a couple of  important ways firstly we all need to be  advocates for our profession  the profession of teaching uh being  teacher educators and especially in  mathematics education  because we are not well understood by  people out there maybe it's because  we're not very good at telling people  what it is that we do and all the good  stuff that we do we need to get better  at talking to people who are not like us  we're good at talking to people who are  like us  but somehow we've got to get better at  talking to those people who want to  criticize us  second thing i think and we need to be  very honest about this is that whenever  there is an inquiry  it does provide an opportunity for us to  reflect on  the quality of our own knowledge and the  quality of our own practice and if  necessary to seek new knowledge  and new practices and to constantly look  for improvement because that is the one  of the hallmarks of a professional  and so the question i'll leave you with  to think about today is how well  prepared do you feel to respond to  student diversity in ways that create  opportunity and equitable outcomes and  how are you going to go about becoming  better prepared for that  so thank you again for the opportunity  and i hope you have a wonderful day
 

